WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.049 --> 00:00:03.090
YUFENG GUO: Hey, did you
know that TensorFlow now

00:00:03.090 --> 00:00:05.310
supports JavaScript?

00:00:05.310 --> 00:00:08.100
Well, if you didn't, now you do.

00:00:08.100 --> 00:00:10.680
Stay tuned to find out
how to run TensorFlow

00:00:10.680 --> 00:00:12.810
on the most widely
used programming

00:00:12.810 --> 00:00:14.532
language in the world.

00:00:14.532 --> 00:00:16.500
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:17.976 --> 00:00:19.944
[BEEPING]

00:00:22.900 --> 00:00:24.930
Welcome to Cloud AI
Adventures, where

00:00:24.930 --> 00:00:28.900
we explore the art, science,
and tools of machine learning.

00:00:28.900 --> 00:00:31.580
My name is Yufeng Guo,
and on this episode,

00:00:31.580 --> 00:00:34.620
we'll unpack
TensorFlow.js a little bit

00:00:34.620 --> 00:00:38.670
and show you some of the amazing
things that you can do with it.

00:00:38.670 --> 00:00:41.160
I'm sure you'll come up
with your own epic use cases

00:00:41.160 --> 00:00:42.340
by the end of this.

00:00:42.340 --> 00:00:44.420
So let's dive right in.

00:00:44.420 --> 00:00:49.080
TensorFlow.js lives
at js.tensorflow.org.

00:00:49.080 --> 00:00:52.260
There you can find all the
documentation, tutorials,

00:00:52.260 --> 00:00:55.890
and links to awesome examples.

00:00:55.890 --> 00:00:57.990
The tutorials really
do a great job

00:00:57.990 --> 00:01:00.010
of layering on
top of each other,

00:01:00.010 --> 00:01:02.610
building up your
TensorFlow.js skills.

00:01:02.610 --> 00:01:06.180
Start at the top of the page and
work your way down one by one.

00:01:06.180 --> 00:01:09.330
Along the way, you'll train a
convolutional neural network

00:01:09.330 --> 00:01:13.210
in your browser and
create a Pac-Man game.

00:01:13.210 --> 00:01:18.040
Now, you can play with data
you record with your webcam.

00:01:18.040 --> 00:01:20.910
So what else have people
made with TensorFlow.js?

00:01:20.910 --> 00:01:24.000
Well, for one,
there's PoseNet, which

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:26.820
does a pretty impressive
job at identifying

00:01:26.820 --> 00:01:29.330
your position in an image.

00:01:29.330 --> 00:01:31.890
OK, so that silliness
aside, what else

00:01:31.890 --> 00:01:34.410
is TensorFlow.js good for?

00:01:34.410 --> 00:01:38.010
Well, you can import your
TensorFlow and Keras models

00:01:38.010 --> 00:01:40.080
directly into the browser now.

00:01:40.080 --> 00:01:43.250
This enables you to train
complex models with large data

00:01:43.250 --> 00:01:43.770
sets--

00:01:43.770 --> 00:01:45.270
just like you're used to doing--

00:01:45.270 --> 00:01:49.030
and then bring those models
straight into the browser.

00:01:49.030 --> 00:01:50.610
And we'll actually
see an example

00:01:50.610 --> 00:01:53.250
of that in a future
episode of AI Adventures.

00:01:53.250 --> 00:01:55.360
So keep your eye out.

00:01:55.360 --> 00:01:57.160
Now, if you really
want to get fancy,

00:01:57.160 --> 00:02:00.750
you can take an imported
model in TensorFlow.js

00:02:00.750 --> 00:02:02.920
and then further train it.

00:02:02.920 --> 00:02:06.330
So imagine loading in an
existing state of the art

00:02:06.330 --> 00:02:10.440
research model, then adding
some of your own training data

00:02:10.440 --> 00:02:12.900
to specialize that
model in some way,

00:02:12.900 --> 00:02:16.060
maybe with images
from the webcam.

00:02:16.060 --> 00:02:18.750
So some of you may
be wondering, hey,

00:02:18.750 --> 00:02:21.510
what's the relationship
between TensorFlow.js

00:02:21.510 --> 00:02:25.300
and this other
library, deeplearn.js?

00:02:25.300 --> 00:02:28.860
Well, TensorFlow.js is a whole
ecosystem of JavaScript tools

00:02:28.860 --> 00:02:30.050
for machine learning.

00:02:30.050 --> 00:02:33.840
And it actually evolved
from deeplearn.js.

00:02:33.840 --> 00:02:37.680
That portion is now known
as TensorFlow.js Core.

00:02:37.680 --> 00:02:40.300
The rest of
TensorFlow.js features

00:02:40.300 --> 00:02:42.570
additional
functionality and tools

00:02:42.570 --> 00:02:47.410
to help make doing machine
learning in JavaScript easier.

00:02:47.410 --> 00:02:50.560
Another big question around
doing machine learning

00:02:50.560 --> 00:02:53.740
with JavaScript is performance.

00:02:53.740 --> 00:02:58.870
Anecdotally, for predictions,
TensorFlow.js with WebGL

00:02:58.870 --> 00:03:01.990
is about one and half
to two times slower

00:03:01.990 --> 00:03:06.460
than TensorFlow using
Python with AVX enabled.

00:03:06.460 --> 00:03:10.150
On the training side, we've
seen that large models train

00:03:10.150 --> 00:03:13.930
actually 10 or even 15
times slower in a browser

00:03:13.930 --> 00:03:16.630
compared with
TensorFlow on Python.

00:03:16.630 --> 00:03:20.650
So while it's unlikely that
TensorFlow.js will completely

00:03:20.650 --> 00:03:24.280
replace the raw performance
of server side training,

00:03:24.280 --> 00:03:26.650
there are some
amazing experiences

00:03:26.650 --> 00:03:30.730
to be created via client
side machine learning.

00:03:30.730 --> 00:03:33.730
This was just the quick
overview of TensorFlow.js.

00:03:33.730 --> 00:03:36.830
I was super excited at the fact
that this is out in the wild.

00:03:36.830 --> 00:03:39.160
So I really needed
to get this to you

00:03:39.160 --> 00:03:41.650
so you can get started
as soon as possible.

00:03:41.650 --> 00:03:43.840
We'll spend some more
time with TensorFlow.js

00:03:43.840 --> 00:03:46.240
in future episodes
and look at some tips

00:03:46.240 --> 00:03:49.220
and tricks in more detail.

